---
title: Roast turkey
servings: 10
tags: [american, thanksgiving, holiday, fall]
cuisine: american
time: 2 hours 20 minutes
source: Juicy Roast Turkey (Dry Brined) by Nagi Maehashi, RecipeTin Eats, https://www.recipetineats.com/genius-easy-juicy-roast-turkey-dry-brined/
---

> Start two days ahead: the salt needs 2 to 3 days on the bird in the fridge.
> Make the gravy from Turkey gravy (sauces) in the roasting pan while the bird rests.

== Dry brine ==

Mix the @kosher salt{2.5%tbsp}, @dried thyme{2%tsp}, @paprika{1%tsp}, @garlic powder{1%tsp} and @black pepper{1/2%tsp} in a #small bowl{}.

Take the giblets out of the @whole turkey{10%lb}(thawed, not pre-brined) and pat it dry inside and out.

Rub the salt mix inside the cavity, underneath and all over the skin, putting most of it on the breast.

Wrap the bird tightly in #plastic wrap{} and refrigerate it breast side down for ~{24%hours}. Turn it breast side up and keep it chilled for the rest of the 2 days.

== Roast ==

Unwrap the turkey, pat it dry and leave it out ~{30%minutes}. Heat the oven to 425°F (220°C).

Put the @garlic{2%heads}(halved crosswise) and the @yellow onion{1}(halved) in a #roasting pan{} with one bunch of the @fresh herbs{2%small bunches}(sage, rosemary, thyme, parsley) and set a #roasting rack{} over them. Keep two of the garlic halves back for the cavity.

Fill the cavity with the other herb bunch, the two garlic halves and @unsalted butter{45%g}(melted). Tie the drumsticks together if you like.

Set the turkey breast side down on the rack. Brush it with some of the remaining @unsalted butter{105%g}(melted) and pour the @dry white wine{1.5%cups} into the pan.

Roast ~{30%minutes}. Turn it breast side up, brush with a little more butter and lower the oven to 325°F (165°C). Roast ~{45%minutes}.

Stir the @fresh sage{1%tsp}(finely chopped), @fresh rosemary{1%tsp}(finely chopped), @fresh thyme{1%tsp}(finely chopped) and @garlic{3%cloves}(minced) into the rest of the butter. Brush it over the bird.

Roast about ~{30%minutes} more, until the thickest part between breast and leg reads 165°F (75°C). Lay foil loosely on top if it browns too fast.

> Allow about 12 minutes per pound in all, high-heat stage included: 1 3/4 to 2 hours for 10 lb, 2 1/4 hours for 12 lb, 2 1/2 hours for 14 lb.

Lift the turkey onto a board, cover loosely with #foil{} and rest ~{30%minutes} before carving.
